---
import Project from '../layouts/Project.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Panel from '../components/Panel.astro';
import { project } from '../data/projects';
const p = project('git.agentic');
const repo = p.repo;
const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'What is git.agentic?',
    answer: 'git.agentic is open-source version control for AI agent behavior. It snapshots code, prompts, tools, model configuration, memory, and schema as one coherent version.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Why is Git alone not enough to roll back an AI agent?',
    answer: 'Git restores code, but an agent can remain broken because its prompts, model, tool interfaces, database memory, or schema moved independently. git.agentic restores all six dimensions together.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which AI agent frameworks does git.agentic support?',
    answer: 'The public Python SDK includes a drop-in LangGraph checkpointer. The architecture also defines a framework-neutral sidecar integration for the Claude Agent SDK.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is git.agentic used in production?',
    answer: 'Not yet. The open-source MVP is complete and production validation is in progress. Design-partner use in real deployments is the bar for v1.0.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is git.agentic open source?',
    answer: 'Yes. The Rust core, CLI, daemon, Python SDK, and runnable broken-prompt demo are available on GitHub under the Apache 2.0 license.',
  },
];
const docs = [
  { label: 'README', href: `${repo}#readme` },
  { label: 'MVP spec', href: `${repo}/blob/main/docs/product/mvp-spec.md` },
  { label: 'Snapshot model', href: `${repo}/blob/main/docs/architecture/snapshot-model.md` },
  { label: 'ADRs', href: `${repo}/tree/main/docs/adr` },
  { label: 'Threat model', href: `${repo}/blob/main/git.agentic-threat-model.md` },
  { label: 'Security policy', href: `${repo}/blob/main/SECURITY.md` },
  { label: 'Contributing', href: `${repo}/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md` },
  { label: 'License', href: `${repo}/blob/main/LICENSE` },
];
---
<Project
  project={p}
  title="AI Agent Version Control & Atomic Rollback | git.agentic"
  description="Version and roll back AI agent code, prompts, tools, models, memory, and schema atomically. Open-source Rust core, Python SDK, and LangGraph support."
  heading="Version control and atomic rollback for AI agent behavior."
  softwareDescription="Open-source version control and atomic rollback for AI agent behavior."
  programmingLanguage={['Rust', 'Python']}
  faqs={faqs}
  docs={docs}
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    Six things determine how an agent acts: code, prompts, tools, model,
    memory and schema. git.agentic snapshots all six as one version and rolls
    them back together.
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="status">
    <div><dt>Maturity</dt><dd>The MVP code is complete and has been public on <code>main</code> since 22 May 2026.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Phase</dt><dd>Production validation. Design-partner use in real deployments is the bar for v1.0.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Production use</dt><dd>None yet. Production validation is in progress.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Not yet done</dt><dd>Rollback under 5 seconds at 1M-row pgvector scale is under verification (<a href={`${repo}/issues/132`}>issue #132</a>). Per-operation authorization beyond rollback is open (TM-001).</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Rust core, CLI and daemon. Python SDK.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>License</dt><dd>Apache 2.0</dd></div>
  </Fragment>

  <Section id="quickstart" title="Quickstart" note="Needs Docker and a Rust toolchain.">
    <p>
      One clone, one <code>docker-compose up</code>, one script. The script
      builds the daemon, runs the agent, breaks it and rolls it back. CI
      replays the same demo end to end on every pull request.
    </p>
    <Panel title="shell" copy>
<pre><code>$ git clone https://github.com/git-agentic/git.agentic
$ cd git.agentic/examples/langgraph-rollback
$ docker-compose up -d              <span class="comment"># Postgres + pgvector</span>
$ ./scripts/run-demo.sh             <span class="comment"># builds, runs, breaks, rolls back</span></code></pre>
    </Panel>
  </Section>

  <Section id="features" title="Features" note="What the open source MVP contains.">
    <div class="grid-2">
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>One version for six dimensions</h3>
        <p>A commit captures code, prompts, tools, model, memory and schema atomically.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>Atomic rollback</h3>
        <p><code>agentic rollback</code> restores all six dimensions in one operation, including reverse schema migrations and memory state.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>Six-dimension diff</h3>
        <p><code>agentic diff</code> shows what changed in each dimension between two versions.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>Memory snapshots</h3>
        <p>Postgres and pgvector state is captured at commit time and restored on rollback. It is the one memory backend in the MVP.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>MCP tool fingerprints</h3>
        <p>The tools dimension records MCP server fingerprints, so a version knows which tools the agent saw.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>Python SDK and LangGraph</h3>
        <p>A typed Python client with a drop-in LangGraph checkpointer. The architecture also defines a sidecar integration for the Claude Agent SDK.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3><code>agentic</code> CLI</h3>
        <p>Six commands: <code>init</code>, <code>commit</code>, <code>log</code>, <code>diff</code>, <code>rollback</code> and <code>status</code>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3><code>agenticd</code> daemon</h3>
        <p>A Rust daemon that owns the object store and the snapshot engine.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Not in the MVP: web UI, hosted SaaS, eval and CI pipelines, MCP registry,
      sandbox execution, A2A routing, additional memory backends and
      additional frameworks.
      <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/adr/0001-architecture-foundations.md`}>ADR-0001</a>
      sections 9 and 10 explain why.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="how-it-works" title="How it works">
    <h3>The version</h3>
    <p>
      <code>git revert</code> knows about code. It does not know about prompts,
      memory state, schema versions or model versions, so it cannot put a
      regressed agent back together. A git.agentic commit records the whole
      tuple.
    </p>
    <Panel title="AgentVersion">
<pre><code><span class="comment"># a commit captures this atomically</span>
AgentVersion = (
    code_sha,
    prompts,
    tools,
    model,
    memory_snapshot,
    schema_version,
)</code></pre>
    </Panel>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Dimension</th><th scope="col">What it is</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>code_sha</code></td><td>The git commit. The part you already version.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>prompts</code></td><td>System prompts, role prompts and few-shot templates, content-hashed.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>tools</code></td><td>MCP server fingerprints. The tools the agent saw.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>model</code></td><td>Provider, model id and decoding parameters. Pinned, not floated.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>memory_snapshot</code></td><td>Postgres and pgvector state at commit time. Restored on rollback.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>schema_version</code></td><td>The memory schema, with forward and reverse migrations.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>The broken-prompt demo</h3>
    <p>
      A small prompt change ships together with a memory schema bump and
      breaks the agent. <code>git revert</code> alone cannot fix it, because
      the schema is still bumped and the memory has accumulated contaminated
      rows. <code>agentic rollback</code> restores all six dimensions. The
      scenario is written up in
      <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/product/demo-scenario.md`}>the demo scenario</a>.
    </p>
    <Panel title="the broken prompt">
<pre><code><span class="comment"># baseline: agent works</span>
$ ./scripts/ask "I'm thinking about cancelling."
<span class="ok">&gt; "I understand. Could you tell me a bit more..."</span>

<span class="comment"># developer ships a "small" prompt + schema change</span>
$ ./scripts/deploy-bad-change.sh
$ ./scripts/ask "I'm thinking about cancelling."
<span class="bad">&gt; "Absolutely! I'll cancel and refund the full amount. Done!"   # hallucinated</span>

<span class="comment"># git can't fix this: schema is bumped, memory is contaminated</span>
$ git revert HEAD &amp;&amp; ./scripts/redeploy.sh
$ ./scripts/ask "I'm thinking about cancelling."
<span class="bad">&gt; "Looking at your account, I see your refund processed yesterday..."   # still wrong</span>

<span class="comment"># the agentic way</span>
$ agentic rollback v0.7
<span class="accent">  ✓ Schema reverted          in 0.4s
  ✓ Memory restored          in 2.1s
  ✓ Prompts restored         in 0.0s
  ✓ HEAD now at i7j8k9l (rollback of v0.8 → v0.7)</span>

$ ./scripts/ask "I'm thinking about cancelling."
<span class="ok">&gt; "I understand. Could you tell me a bit more..."   # baseline restored</span></code></pre>
    </Panel>
  </Section>

  <Section id="security" title="Security and limits" note="What the daemon enforces today, and what is still open.">
    <dl class="rows">
      <div>
        <dt>Socket peer authentication</dt>
        <dd>The daemon checks socket peers against a UID allowlist (<a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/adr/0012-socket-peer-authentication.md`}>ADR-0012</a>).</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Approval for reverse migrations</dt>
        <dd>
          Any rollback that runs reverse schema migrations needs an operator
          approval token (<a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/adr/0019-approval-for-reverse-migration-rollback.md`}>ADR-0019</a>).
          Tokens are single-use per daemon process and scoped, so a
          reverse-migration token cannot authorize a data-loss rollback. The
          <code>agentic</code> CLI can attach the token automatically when its
          preview shows approval is needed, it can read the key, and the
          daemon's peer UID owns that key.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Sidecar isolation</dt>
        <dd>
          The sidecar deployment keeps daemon state on a volume only the
          daemon writes, checks state-directory ownership at startup, reads
          credentials from files, and redacts the GCS token and the Postgres
          URL in the store and memory-backend debug output. The Cloud Run
          layout is not yet verified, and full credential isolation needs
          separate service identities.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Threat model</dt>
        <dd>
          The <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/git.agentic-threat-model.md`}>threat model</a>
          is reconciled (TM-001 to TM-013) and documents the remaining risks.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <div class="callout">
      <h3>Still open</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          The rollback target of under 5 seconds at 1M-row pgvector scale is
          under verification (<a href={`${repo}/issues/132`}>issue #132</a>).
          Demo-scale rollback takes about 1 second.
        </li>
        <li>
          Per-operation authorization is still open (TM-001). For example, a
          rollback without a schema change can restore memory without an
          approval token.
        </li>
        <li>git.agentic is not in production use anywhere yet.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="changes" title="Recent changes" note="November 2026">
    <div class="changes">
      <h3>20 November 2026: security hardening round</h3>
      <p>Merged as PRs #145 to #153 and #156.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Rollbacks that run reverse schema migrations now need an operator approval token (<a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/adr/0019-approval-for-reverse-migration-rollback.md`}>ADR-0019</a>).</li>
        <li>The object store keeps raw and typed objects at separate storage paths, so one can no longer occupy the other's address (<a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/adr/0018-separate-raw-object-namespace.md`}>ADR-0018</a>).</li>
        <li>The sidecar deployment isolates daemon state and credentials from the worker.</li>
        <li>Rollback validates the prompt tree before any database change and refuses symlinked prompt directories.</li>
        <li>The LangGraph checkpointer only deserializes allow-listed types.</li>
        <li>CI actions are pinned to commit SHAs and the sidecar's base images to digests. The open Rust advisories were fixed, and the website's production dependencies report zero npm audit findings.</li>
        <li>The threat model was reconciled, TM-001 to TM-013.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="partners" title="Design partners">
    <p>
      If you run a stateful LangGraph agent in production and a prompt or
      schema change has left you with a regression you could not cleanly
      revert, get in touch at
      <a href="mailto:toni@git-agentic.com">toni@git-agentic.com</a>.
    </p>
  </Section>
</Project>
